  <!-- Skip Link -->
  <main id="main-content" class="seo-page"><div class="seo-answer"><strong>Direct answer:</strong> OCD, panic disorder and PTSD at Mind Grace Neuropsychiatric Clinic are treated with exposure-based therapies such as ERP and CBT, combined with medication when required, following internationally recommended protocols.</div>
<section class="seo-hero"><p class="seo-kicker">Anxiety and trauma-related concerns</p><h1>When thoughts, fear, or memories take over</h1><p class="seo-lead">Intrusive thoughts, compulsions, panic, avoidance, nightmares, or trauma-related distress can be exhausting. A confidential assessment can clarify possible next steps.</p><div class="seo-actions"><a class="btn" href="/book">Request support</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="/therapy">Explore therapy</a></div></section><section class="seo-section"><h2>Support is individual</h2><div class="seo-grid"><article class="seo-card"><h3>Understand the pattern</h3><p>The clinician may explore triggers, thoughts, behaviours, physical sensations, safety, and impact on daily life.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Discuss options</h3><p>Options may include therapy, coping strategies, medication discussion, or referral depending on the assessment.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Move at a safe pace</h3><p>Care planning should respect readiness, privacy, culture, relationships, and current safety.</p></article></div><div class="seo-note"><strong>Immediate danger:</strong> This clinic is not an emergency service. Call 112 or attend the nearest emergency department if needed.</div></section>    
  <section class='seo-section'><h2>Start with the concern and its context</h2><p>OCD, panic and post-traumatic stress can affect people differently, and several factors can contribute to similar experiences. A website cannot diagnose an individual. If a concern is persistent, distressing or interfering with daily life, note when it occurs, what tends to trigger or ease it, and what parts of work, school, relationships or routines are affected. Include what has helped, what has not, and any questions you want answered.</p><p>During a clinical conversation, the provider may ask about the course of the concern, sleep, physical health, medicines, previous care and relevant life circumstances. You can ask why a question matters and how information will be used. There is no need to prepare a complete file before requesting a first appointment. The clinician can explain whether a medical consultation, psychological assessment, therapy or referral is a reasonable starting point.</p><h2>Assessment and care planning</h2><p>Assessment considers more than one symptom or screening result. Depending on the question, it may involve a clinical history, structured questions, records or input from other settings with appropriate consent. Findings can clarify strengths, needs and uncertainty as well as diagnoses. The clinician should explain what the assessment can and cannot establish and how the findings shape practical next steps. See <a href='/assessments'>assessment options</a> and <a href='/process'>first-visit preparation</a>.</p><p>Care may include follow-up, therapy, a medication discussion, practical supports, further assessment or referral, depending on the individual. No single approach is appropriate for everyone, and treatment response varies. Ask about the purpose of a suggested option, expected benefits, possible risks, alternatives and how progress will be reviewed. Do not change a prescribed treatment based only on online information.</p><h2>Choosing between psychiatry and therapy</h2><p>A psychiatrist is a medical doctor who can evaluate mental-health concerns in the context of physical health and discuss medication. Therapy offers psychological support with a qualified clinician within their professional scope. These options may complement one another. Read about <a href='/psychiatry'>psychiatry consultations</a>, <a href='/therapy'>clinical psychology and therapy</a> and the <a href='/psychiatrist-vs-psychologist'>difference between care roles</a>. If you are unsure, the clinic can answer a general routing question without asking you to send a detailed clinical history.</p><h2>Safety and next steps</h2><p>For immediate danger or a medical emergency, call 112 or go to the nearest emergency department; Mind Grace does not provide emergency care. For routine support in Greater Noida, check <a href='/fees'>fees</a>, <a href='/location'>directions</a> and <a href='/book'>appointment requests</a>. A submitted request is not a confirmed appointment until the clinic contacts you and agrees a date and time.</p></section></main>
</Layout>
